ion_fig1.svg":{"alt":"","sha256":"a2d09572bb1c276ab5a2d96ade89ca39b7a19de6c0b1f27cb672a0dec0274e79","source":{"alt":"","url":"https://live.poshenloh.com/images/past-contests/amc12/2001/8.svg"},"url":"https://live.poshenloh.com/images/past-contests/amc12/2001/8.svg"}},"id":"2001_AMC_12_Q08","knowledgeGraph":{"assessedIds":["cnm_258"],"contentHash":"3bc404fd02ec86ada1ba593ef233254c13890ca1e08a73e6c80ce140e7e31b07","flags":[],"reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p190","cnm_250"],"id":"arc-becomes-circumference","label":"Track the sector's radius and arc","methodDirectNodeIds":{"arc-becomes-circumference":["cnm_258","cnm_256"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_258","cnm_256","cnm_p190","cnm_250"]}],"status":"mapped","supportIds":["cnm_256","cnm_p190"],"version":"1.0.0"},"level":"AMC 12","number":8,"question":"Which of the cones below can be formed from a \\(252^\\circ\\) sector of a circle of radius \\(10\\) by aligning the two straight sides?\n\n[Figure 1](figures/2001_AMC_12_Q08_question_fig1.svg)","questionType":"mixed","rejectedAnswers":[],"responseType":"choice","schemaVersion":"3.0","skill":[],"solution":"When the straight sides are joined, the sector's center becomes the cone's vertex. Thus the sector radius becomes the slant height, which is \\(10\\).\n\nThe curved edge wraps once around the base, so its length equals the base circumference. If \\(r\\) is the base radius, then\n\\[\n2\\pi r=\\frac{252}{360}(2\\pi\\cdot10)=14\\pi.\n\\]\nDividing by \\(2\\pi\\) gives \\(r=7\\). The required cone therefore has slant height \\(10\\) and base radius \\(7\\), which is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).","solutionAvailability":"present","solutionMethods":[{"directNodeIds":["cnm_258","cnm_256"],"explanation":"When the straight sides are joined, the sector's center becomes the cone's vertex. Thus the sector radius becomes the slant height, which is \\(10\\).\n\nThe curved edge wraps once around the base, so its length equals the base circumference. If \\